-- EXPECT-ERROR: `Cap(IO.FileRead)` used in module `NestedCap` but `IO.FileRead` is not declared in `needs`
-- `needs` coverage, CAPABILITY NESTED IN A CONTAINER (2026-08-05).
--
-- reject/t148 puts the capability directly in a record field. This one buries
-- it one constructor deep, so it passes only if the type walk RECURSES
-- through type arguments rather than pattern-matching the field type against
-- a bare `Cap(_)`. A shallow check reads `List(_)`, sees no capability, and
-- reports nothing.
--
-- This is also the shape a real evasion would take: `List(Cap(X))`,
-- `Option(Cap(X))` or `Map(String, Cap(X))` are how a capability actually
-- travels in a data structure, and none of them are literally `Cap(X)`.
--
-- (An ALIAS right-hand side — the third arm of `type_def` — is walked by
-- Cap_surface_ty.caps_in_type_def for completeness but has no reject witness
-- here, because it has no source spelling: `type T = Cap(IO.FileRead)` parses
-- as a VARIANT declaring a constructor named `Cap`, not as an alias, and
-- `type T = (Cap(IO.FileRead), Int)` does not parse at all. Verified
-- 2026-08-05; the coverage is defensive, not witnessed.)
mod NestedCap do
  needs IO.Console

  type Bag = { label : String, items : List(Cap(IO.FileRead)) }

  fn main() do
    println("a capability this module never declared, one level down")
  end
end
